WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:06.140
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level three.

2
00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:09.617
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.917 --> 00:00:17.527
Unit twenty-three: Public Opinion and Polls. Today you will report poll results with proportion phrases.

4
00:00:17.827 --> 00:00:20.468
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.433 --> 00:00:24.434
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.283 --> 00:00:26.412
Opinion poll.

7
00:00:27.417 --> 00:00:30.670
Opinion poll means a survey that measures what people think.

8
00:00:31.283 --> 00:00:33.620
The opinion poll showed a tight race.

9
00:00:34.217 --> 00:00:37.566
Your turn. Say: Opinion poll.

10
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:41.831
Opinion poll.

11
00:00:42.583 --> 00:00:43.480
Survey.

12
00:00:44.483 --> 00:00:48.120
Survey means a set of questions asked to find out people's views.

13
00:00:48.717 --> 00:00:50.995
The survey reached two thousand households.

14
00:00:51.583 --> 00:00:53.945
Your turn. Say: Survey.

15
00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:57.743
Survey.

16
00:00:58.483 --> 00:00:59.357
Sample.

17
00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:04.335
Sample means a small group chosen to represent a larger one.

18
00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:07.553
The sample included people of every age.

19
00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:10.291
Your turn. Say: Sample.

20
00:01:13.133 --> 00:01:14.134
Sample.

21
00:01:14.883 --> 00:01:16.117
Margin of error.

22
00:01:17.117 --> 00:01:20.556
Margin of error means the range by which a poll result may be wrong.

23
00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:23.707
A margin of error of three points is common.

24
00:01:24.317 --> 00:01:27.050
Your turn. Say: Margin of error.

25
00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:31.098
Margin of error.

26
00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:32.979
Public opinion.

27
00:01:33.983 --> 00:01:37.573
Public opinion means the views held by most people about an issue.

28
00:01:38.183 --> 00:01:40.856
Public opinion moved quickly after the speech.

29
00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:44.265
Your turn. Say: Public opinion.

30
00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:48.415
Public opinion.

31
00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:49.954
Trend.

32
00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:54.192
Trend means a general direction in which something is changing.

33
00:01:54.783 --> 00:01:57.224
The trend shows growing support for reform.

34
00:01:57.817 --> 00:02:00.179
Your turn. Say: Trend.

35
00:02:03.033 --> 00:02:03.721
Trend.

36
00:02:04.483 --> 00:02:05.705
Question wording.

37
00:02:06.717 --> 00:02:10.307
Question wording means the exact words used to ask a survey question.

38
00:02:10.917 --> 00:02:12.823
Question wording can hide bias.

39
00:02:13.417 --> 00:02:15.883
Your turn. Say: Question wording.

40
00:02:18.733 --> 00:02:20.164
Question wording.

41
00:02:20.917 --> 00:02:22.034
Accuracy.

42
00:02:23.050 --> 00:02:26.257
Accuracy means the quality of being correct and exact.

43
00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:29.175
Accuracy depends on a fair sample.

44
00:02:29.783 --> 00:02:32.587
Your turn. Say: Accuracy.

45
00:02:35.433 --> 00:02:36.574
Accuracy.

46
00:02:37.367 --> 00:02:40.611
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:49.931
Visal, a poll report says 'A narrow majority of respondents support the plan, while a significant minority oppose it.' How do I write statistics like this?

48
00:02:51.017 --> 00:03:06.452
Use proportion phrases: the vast majority, a narrow majority, a significant minority, roughly half, two-thirds of, one in five. They are more natural than lists of numbers.

49
00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:09.422
Is there a rule for the verb?

50
00:03:10.517 --> 00:03:25.139
A majority of respondents takes a plural verb: A majority of respondents support it. Percentages follow the noun: 40 percent of voters are undecided, because the noun after 'of' decides the verb.

51
00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:27.994
How can this show accuracy?

52
00:03:29.083 --> 00:03:39.723
Add the margin of error: 52 percent, plus or minus three points, means the majority is not certain. Good reporting gives both the figure and its limits.

53
00:03:40.867 --> 00:03:42.553
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:43.417 --> 00:03:48.679
Grammar focus: Proportion phrases: a narrow majority and a significant minority.

55
00:03:48.979 --> 00:03:50.212
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:50.917 --> 00:03:53.764
A narrow majority of respondents support the plan.

57
00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:57.938
A significant minority oppose it, roughly one in four.

58
00:03:58.850 --> 00:04:01.755
Forty percent of voters are still undecided.

59
00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:12.191
Use the vast / a narrow majority, a significant minority, two-thirds / one in five + of + plural noun + plural verb. Add the margin of error to show accuracy.

60
00:04:12.950 --> 00:04:16.810
Be careful! Don't say: Forty percent of voters is undecided.

61
00:04:18.767 --> 00:04:22.113
Say: Forty percent of voters are undecided.

62
00:04:22.413 --> 00:04:24.784
The plural noun takes a plural verb.

63
00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:28.485
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:30.580
Reading the Polls.

65
00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:35.341
An opinion poll tries to measure public opinion by asking a sample of people.

66
00:04:36.333 --> 00:04:41.677
A narrow majority of respondents may support a plan, while a significant minority oppose it.

67
00:04:42.667 --> 00:04:47.174
If the margin of error is three points, a lead of two points does not prove a trend.

68
00:04:48.167 --> 00:04:53.162
Question wording matters too, because a leading question can push respondents towards one answer.

69
00:04:54.167 --> 00:04:58.964
Accuracy depends on a fair sample; a sample that leaves out one group can mislead.

70
00:05:00.067 --> 00:05:04.080
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:04.917 --> 00:05:07.741
A narrow majority of respondents blank the plan.

72
00:05:08.041 --> 00:05:11.764
A, supports. B, support. C, is supporting.

73
00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:17.671
The answer is B: support.

74
00:05:18.817 --> 00:05:21.582
The possible error in a poll's result is the blank.

75
00:05:21.882 --> 00:05:25.698
A, margin of error. B, sample. C, trend.

76
00:05:29.233 --> 00:05:31.303
The answer is A: margin of error.

77
00:05:32.450 --> 00:05:35.715
A small group asked to represent everyone is a blank.

78
00:05:36.015 --> 00:05:39.645
A, survey. B, trend. C, sample.

79
00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:45.362
The answer is C: sample.

80
00:05:46.517 --> 00:05:48.156
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:48.456 --> 00:05:56.382
A, Forty percent of voters is undecided.. B, Forty percent of voters are undecided.. C, Forty percents of voters are undecided..

82
00:05:59.933 --> 00:06:03.674
The answer is B: Forty percent of voters are undecided..

83
00:06:04.817 --> 00:06:09.034
According to the reading, a leading question pushes respondents toward one…

84
00:06:09.334 --> 00:06:13.185
A, answer. B, region. C, page.

85
00:06:16.733 --> 00:06:18.489
The answer is A: answer.

86
00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:23.339
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:24.183 --> 00:06:27.263
How can the wording of a poll question change the results?

88
00:06:36.050 --> 00:06:37.620
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:37.920 --> 00:07:03.916
The wording of a question can change results because words carry feelings and hints. A leading question such as 'Do you agree that this wasteful tax should end?' pushes people to say yes. Order, choices and even a single adjective can change answers. Careful pollsters use neutral language, test questions in advance and publish the exact wording so that readers can judge the result.

90
00:07:04.216 --> 00:07:06.053
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:06.950 --> 00:07:13.480
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and proportion phrases: a narrow majority and a significant minority.

92
00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:18.731
Today's value is evidence-based judgment. Let good evidence guide what you decide.

93
00:07:19.031 --> 00:07:21.811
See you in Unit twenty-four. Goodbye!

94
00:07:22.111 --> 00:07:25.750
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

